title: note_subcaptain_riddle
---
Halyna kept me sane through every storm. Grisha will captain his own ship one day. Anya still waits for me on the dock.
===

title: note_c01
---
Your calm is the first tool. Your silence is the second.
===

title: note_c02
---
NAVIGATION MAP — EL MARINERA
Deck B: Living quarters and command areas.
Deck C: Technical and operational areas.
Access to each area is regulated by your rank credential.
Unmarked areas are restricted sectors.
RESTRICTED AREA: access by direct authorization only.
Do not ask your superior for information about these areas.
===

title: note_c03
---
KNOW YOUR ROLE ON EL MARINERA
SHEEP — General crew. Access: exterior, balconies, common areas. Your safety is our priority.
HORSE — Technical staff. Access: power rooms, water room, technical corridors. Your key opens what you need. Not what you want.
DOG — Security and command. Access: cabins, mess hall, security areas. Your duty is to keep order. Order keeps you.
PIG — Management and sciences. Access: laboratory, clinics, command cubicles. The ship's mission depends on your work. Do not interrupt it.
Each rank has what it needs. Each rank is necessary.
===

title: note_c04
---
Water is the blood that keeps this ship alive.
Keep it moving. Keep it clean. Keep it quiet.
===

title: note_c05
---
Power does not think — power obeys.
The current never rests, and neither should your loyalty.
===

title: note_c06
---
You are not individuals. You are the soul of El Marinera.
There is no soul without the ship.
===

title: note_d01
---
December 9th
Routine check done. The electricity room's mine to look after -- breaker panel, backup generator, wiring conduits. Everything read normal.
Bola de Nieve settled in fine at the Cat Room. Left him the old blanket and a bowl of water. Good cat. Doesn't bother anyone.
Today's rations tasted different. A bit metallic. Probably the preservatives in the new boxes. Not the first time they've switched suppliers without saying.
<page>
December 12th
Bola de Nieve's doing fine. Purrs when I come in. Good sign.
The water system in Deck C's corridors keeps making that noise. Already reported it twice. Nobody checks it. If it were a pressure leak it would've shown by now.
Rations came late today. The chef said there's an inventory problem. Didn't really follow the explanation. He looked uncomfortable when I asked.
The strange taste is still there. I'll get used to it.
<page>
December 20th
Something's off with Petrov. He used to be the most careful one on shift. Today he let a pressure reading slide that he would've logged three times before. Asked if he was okay. He looked at me like he didn't understand the question.
Wrote "switched off" in my observation notebook. Don't know how else to put it.
Bola de Nieve was restless this evening. Pacing in circles. Cats notice things.
Went up to the comms room to see if they could fix my handheld radio. Ended up staying a while, just listening to the guys work. There's something almost fun about Morse -- the way it sounds, the rhythm of it. Might actually learn it one of these days. Good way to hide a message, these days.
===

title: note_d02
---
December 25th
Bola de Nieve isn't in the Cat Room.
I checked the whole sector. The blanket is there. The bowl is there. He isn't.
Someone took him out. There's no other explanation. The Cat Room door has a lock and only we Horses have access. That narrows it down.
I'll find him.
Merry Christmas.
<page>
December 28th
I was looking for Bola de Nieve in the Deck C corridors when I noticed something.
There are people who aren't switched off like Petrov and the others. They move differently. They talk normally. One of them has a Pig badge and eats somewhere else, not in the general mess hall.
The difference is the food. It has to be.
The ones who eat from the mess hall change. The ones who don't eat from the mess hall don't change.
I need to understand what's in the rations.
<page>
January 1st
I tried to reach the Lab HallWay. There's a door with a black reader. My red badge doesn't work. It only works in the technical areas, as always.
It's not fair that there are doors nobody explains. I have a right to know what's happening on my ship.
I left extra food in the Cat Room in case Bola de Nieve comes back. Maybe he left on his own and found another way.
New year. Same ship.
===

title: note_d03
---
January 3rd
I didn't sleep well.
This morning I saw someone in corridor C-2 use a black card on a door that isn't on the maintenance plans. A door that, according to the plans, is a wall.
I waited forty minutes in the corridor. When he came out, the card was still in the pocket of his lab coat. White. He was a Pig.
There are doors that don't exist on the plans. There are doors that do exist.
<page>
January 5th
I have the card.
I know I shouldn't have taken it but sometimes you have to act even if the rulebook says otherwise because the rulebook doesn't know what's going on here and I do or almost do and that's why I took the card and hid it under Bola de Nieve's blanket in the Cat Room where nobody will look because nobody knows the cat is mine.
Tomorrow I'll use it. Or the day after. When the moment comes.
<page>
January 6th
I saw them go in.
Scientists. White coats. They went into the Lab HallWay through the door that doesn't exist on the plans. Three of them. One was carrying something.
There's something behind that wall that isn't part of the ship I know.
I have the card.
Tomorrow I go in.
===

title: note_d04
---
No date
I went in.
I saw what's back there.
I don't know how to write it. I don't have the words for what I saw and the words I have don't match what I remember and I remember fragments not everything that happened inside but what I remember is enough to know that
I'm going to the Cat Room first.
Bola de Nieve has to be there.
Then I'll come back and write everything down.
===

title: note_d05
---
December 11th.
Quick log: the electricity machine isn't working right.
The first row of switches won't light up, but that doesn't bother me — I already know the password.
What I need to remember is that the second and third rows, together, have to match the first.
===

title: note_l01
---
OBSERVATION LOG — SUBJECT KRK-01 — Session 01 — November 18th
Initial evaluation completed.
Baseline established: logic 94%, motor coordination 91%, stress tolerance 87%.
First dose administered: 0.3ml standard KRK-NL solution.
No observable effects in the first six hours.
The subject cooperated without resistance. First documentable response expected in 72-96 hours.
General condition: optimal.
<page>
OBSERVATION LOG — SUBJECT KRK-01 — Session 03 — November 24th
First documented improvement.
The subject shows a notable reduction in reactive responses. More direct, less elaborate communication. Follows instructions without delay or question.
Increased appetite: requests an additional ration at the end of the session.
Conclusion: the program is showing a positive early response.
<page>
OBSERVATION LOG — SUBJECT KRK-01 — Session 05 — November 30th
The subject's vocabulary has simplified. Shorter sentences. Less use of subordinate clauses.
I attribute it to accumulated navigation fatigue.
Logic test: drop from 94% to 78%. Manual tests: 91% stable.
A decline worth considering, though not alarming. The protocol will continue without modifications.
<page>
OBSERVATION LOG — SUBJECT KRK-01 — Session 07 — December 6th
The subject ate lunch without cutlery. He did not seem to notice the change in his own behavior.
Cognitive tests: 31%. Manual tests: 88%.
The dissociation between cognitive and motor functions is notable.
Recommendation: continue. This dissociation is precisely what the program seeks to document.
<page>
OBSERVATION LOG — SUBJECT KRK-01 — Session 09 — December 12th
The subject refuses the standard ration. He only accepts the protein concentrate from tray B.
I do not understand the mechanism behind this preference. It could be olfactory, it could be behavioral. It is not in the literature.
So as not to interrupt the observations, I adjusted the subject's diet to his preference.
Science requires adaptation.
<page>
OBSERVATION LOG — SUBJECT KRK-01 — Session 11 — no date
The subject can no longer complete cognitive tests. He does not respond to verbal questions. He only reacts to the presence of food.
During today's session he positioned himself behind me and stayed there for forty minutes. He did nothing. He only waited.
I have decided not to delay the ration at the start of the session again.
<page>
OBSERVATION LOG — SUBJECT KRK-01 — Session 12 — no date
The subject attacked me when I entered without a ration.
There is a wound on the right forearm. Superficial.
I am not going to report it. It was a protocol error on my part: I entered without the standard ration. It will not happen again.
The subject is not at fault.
<page>
OBSERVATION LOG — SUBJECT KRK-01 — Session 13 — no date
The subject also refuses the protein concentrate.
He stays in the northeast corner of the room. Staring at the wall.
He does not react to the presence of food. He does not react to my presence.
I have left the room.
I have no more observations to record today.
===

title: note_l02
---
CLOSING NOTE — KRK-01 LOG
The person in charge of this log was found in the Observation Room on December 23rd.
Subject KRK-01 was present in the room.
No further records of this subject are required.
The program continues with other subjects.
===

title: note_t01
---
My love,
Writing this real late, everybody's asleep already but I can't get my eyes to close tonight.
Chef found some boxes down in the storeroom today. Army rations, still sealed, date on 'em is way off yet. We ate good tonight, first time in a long while. 
Real meat. Bread still warm out the oven.
Made me think somebody's looking out for us out here. Somebody up top knows we exist, at least.
Dmitri says the food tastes off. Told him it's just 'cause we ain't used to eating this good no more.
<page>
Portions were smaller today though.
Chef says it's gotta be that way, needs to last us. Nobody asked him how long for.
Me and Voronin walked out the mess hall and just looked at each other, didn't say nothing. 
Don't know why that stuck with me the way it did. Guess it was the first sign, I just didn't know it yet.
How's Sil doing? Is she eating alright? I think about her every day. Every single day.
Miss you both. Write back when you can.
===

title: note_t02
---
Somebody got into the cabin and took the stash I had under the bunk.
Only two people it could've been. Been bunking with 'em for months now.
Not saying nothing. Ain't no use saying nothing.
From now on I'm keeping what's left somewhere else.
===

title: note_t03
---
Aleksei stopped talking.
Used to tell stories in the mess hall about his daughter. Said she was turning five. Said he promised her a dog.
Now he just comes in, eats fast, and leaves.
Guess it's the hunger. Does something different to each of us.
===

title: note_t04
---
Repin and Zakharov got into it in the mess hall today.
Came aboard together. Used to be friends.
Over the last portion off the center plate. The one nobody touches 'cause it's supposed to be for everyone.
Nobody stepped in. Nobody said a word.
After that they ate on opposite ends of the table. Like the other one wasn't even there. Like they'd never been friends at all.
===

title: note_t05
---
I eat facing the wall now.
So nobody sees how much I've got left.
I know Dobrovolsky watches my plate. Caught him at it. Started noticing it about three days ago.
Don't know when I started caring more about what's on my plate than the person sitting across from me.
Guess it happened gradual.
===

title: note_t06
---
Mirov stopped coming to the mess hall.
Found him this morning in corridor B-3, just standing there facing the wall. Not doing anything. Just standing.
Talked to him. He looked at me.
His eyes were wrong somehow. Like he was seeing me but not looking at what I was.
Went to the mess hall alone.
===

title: note_t07
---
i'm hungry
today's ration wasn't enough
i don't understand why i'm so hungry if i ate
i'm hungry
===

title: note_v01
---
Okay... I don't really know why I'm recording this. I just need to say it out loud, I think.
Adrian is interesting. Genuinely. The way he talks, it's — I don't know, I could just sit there and listen to him for hours. Charming isn't even the word for it.
The ship scared me, before. All of it, the size, the sound it makes at night. But being near him... I don't feel calmer. If anything I feel more on edge. Like something's off.
<page>
It's been a few days now, and I keep getting this feeling that there's more than one of him. Like there's the Adrian who's talking to me, and then there's someone else underneath that I haven't met yet.
He says he's the chef, but he's barely ever in the kitchen. He's in his room. Always in his room.
And when I can, when he's not looking, I peek at his notes. They don't look like recipes. They look like chemistry. I don't know anything about chemistry, but I know what a recipe looks like, and that's not it.
Something's wrong here. I don't like it. And I really don't like being kept in the dark.
===

title: note_v02
---
Okay— the storm. That storm that hit us two nights ago, I'm still kind of shaking just thinking about it. The whole ship was creaking, I mean really creaking, like it was gonna come apart underneath us.
And Adrian... nothing. Didn't check on me, didn't say a word. Which, fine, whatever, but it's not just that night. He talks to me less every day now. I ask him something and I get, like, three words back. I don't know if he's busy or if he's just done with me, and it bothers me more than I want to admit.
<page>
So I did something about it. I started talking to the sub-captain. A lot. Flirting, honestly — men are easy to fool when they see some good curves, so I figured, if Adrian's not gonna tell me anything, maybe he will.
And it worked. Better than I thought, actually. He let a few things slip — small things, but enough. Enough that I'm almost sure the two of them know something they're not sharing.
<page>
I have a plan. I'm not gonna pretend it's the most... ethical thing I've ever done, but I'm tired. Tired of being ignored, tired of being the last one to know anything on this ship. If this is what it takes to get Adrian to actually look at me again, then fine.
...I wonder what Mom would think of me right now.
===

title: note_v03
---
I went into the laboratory.
Adrian isn't what he said he was. I knew that. Now I know what he is.
I found the records. I found the variant. The difference between standard KRK-NL and the reptilian variant is a matter of threshold. The standard one erases. The variant transforms.
The confrontation has to happen now. Before he decides I'm a problem that needs to be solved.
The only leverage I have left is what I found in that laboratory. The only leverage I have left is me.
Mom was seventeen when she made the first decision that defined her forever. I'm twenty-four.
===

title: note_a01
---
Okay — the mess hall. People are in there constantly, and they actually talk, I mean real conversation, like there's trust between them.
Guard's completely down.
Except when someone with a Dog rank walks in. Then the tone shifts, just like that, every time.
This whole rank system, it's... curious, I'll say that much. 
<page>
But everyone treats it like it's just how things work here, so I'm not gonna waste time picking at it.
Not yet anyway.
Crew's mostly Russian, a few Ukrainians, one Belarusian. None of them trained for this. Easy to read.
I met a woman. Let her come aboard with me.
<page>
She's pretty, sure. But she talks — a lot, more than I need her to.
I didn't bring her up here for conversation.
Most nights I go prepare something in the kitchen. 
That's... that's the phrase I use for it. Works well enough.
<page>
Vanessa's asked me twice now why I go out at night. 
Like there's some other woman on this ship I'd be sneaking off to see.
Gets old. Fast, some days.
===

title: note_a02
---
The supplies — they're gone. Destroyed. First I figured it was the storm, made sense given the timing. 
But the more I look at it, the less that story holds together.
It was Vanessa. Has to be.
Last time we talked she started something. A real scene, in front of people, trying to get the crew on her side. And of course some of them jumped right in — the Pigs, mostly. 
Wasn't hard to see coming. Funny what some men will do for a woman.
<page>
Had to do something. We can't make port, not now, not with everything else in motion.
Found some MERI rations I'd set aside earlier. Not great stuff, I know that. 
So I added... something. A little help, let's call it that. 
Nothing anyone needs the details on.
<page>
Few days in and it's working. People stopped complaining about the taste. 
Back to work, functioning normally.
More docile than before, maybe. But they're compliant. 
That's what matters right now.
<page>
Vanessa's getting more dangerous by the day. She's talking to everyone now. 
Even found out she's sleeping with the Captain.
Bothers me, I won't pretend it doesn't. But it doesn't matter, not really.
Talked to him — asked him to keep an eye on her. Told him I think she's up to something.
===

title: note_a03
---
There have been accidents. A few, now. A fight in the mess hall — two men, over a plate of food. 
One of them bit the other. Bit him. Over a plate.
Two more in the cabins, same week. A man who wouldn't stop pacing until the door gave way.
Another who took what he wanted from his bunkmate and didn't see why that was a problem.
<page>
The same instincts, every time. Hunger. Territory. Rage.
<page>
It's only the upper decks. That's the good news, if there is any. 
The lab is sealed off from all of it, so the operation stays covered. 
Nobody up there has any reason to look down here.
<page>
Contained. That's the word I'm using. Contained.
<page>
I saw Vanessa in the corridor today. Something was off about her. The way she looked at me — too long, too still. 
Like she was measuring me.
I don't understand it. She looked affected. But she never touched the MERI rations. 
She hasn't eaten a single one. I hope so.
<page>
Went down to check the samples tonight. The strain isn't there. I checked the locker. Twice.
...
<page>
If she took it — no. Let me think... If she took it, we could be in real trouble.
I don't know how it reacts outside the lab. I don't know what it does to someone who... 
I don't know what she'd do with it.
I just... don't know.
===

title: note_a04
---
No time to explain this properly, I just need to get it out.
The mission's compromised. I need the [important]doll[/important] from the clock's drawer.
Don't forget — it also shuts off the [important]mechanism[/important].
I have to get back to the lab, grab the USB, and run Protocol SCUTTLE before anyone finds that hallway. 
<page>
Twelve minutes, maybe less if the vents are clear. After that the kitchen stays sealed. Nobody goes back in.
And of course, now, of all times, I can't find the key to my case. 
I've checked twice.
<page>
Vanessa. I don't want it to be her. But it has to be her.
Without that key I can't get back into the kitchen at all. She didn't just take a key.
She bought herself time, and I still don't know what she's planning to do with it.
===

title: note_code
---
Radio code.
Signal's best past the antenna at E4. Don't trust the cable at C2, it's worn through. Spare fuses stay at A5.
<page>
<mspace=0.7em>    A B C D E F<br>0  K T M R B X<br>1  P D W H N L<br>2  S G M V Q J<br>3  Y F O U Z C<br>4  V O R H E S<br>5  A Q U N M F</mspace>
===

title: note_b01
---
FIELD SIGNAL UNIT "BEEPER"

PURPOSE
Short-range audible relay for a pre-arranged three-letter authentication code. 
Standard International Morse. No radio signature, no paper trail — just sound. 
Only for whoever is close enough and built to listen for it.
<page>
OPERATION
1. Hold [important]INPUT[/important] to key a DASH. Tap [important]INPUT[/important] to key a DOT.
2. Three seconds without input locks the current letter and opens the next.
3. Repeat until all three letters of your code are set.
4. Press [important]SEND[/important] to transmit. Any unit within range keyed to that exact code will register it.
<page>
Field units mark their own listening range visibly: 
[important]GREEN[/important] means you're standing where it can hear you.  
[important]RED[/important] — blinking — means you're out of range and it won't pick up a thing. 
Get inside the green before you key [important]SEND[/important].
A wrong code transmits — it simply answers nothing. The unit will not tell you which letter missed.
===

title: note_ph01
---
THE pH SCALE — READ BEFORE HANDLING ANY TANK
pH runs from 0 to 14. 7 is neutral: plain water, harmless. Everything else leans one way or the other, and the further it leans, the harder it bites.
<page>
LOW pH — ACIDS
[important]BELOW 7[/important]. Acids eat metal. Rust, bolts, locks, bars: given time, an acid dissolves them.
Use it when something made of metal has to go. Never on skin, never near anything you want to keep.
<page>
HIGH pH — ALKALINES
[important]ABOVE 7[/important]. Alkalines break down grease and oil, and loosen light rust.
Use them to clean, to free a stuck part, to wipe away years of grime. Mild ones are safe enough. Strong ones still burn.
<page>
Read the label. Check the number. Never mix the two.
===
